From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DjDeveloperr <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 03:40:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: clpc: hand the T8140 GPU floor back 100 ms after
 the PMP stops limiting

The GPU floor stayed released for a second after the PMP last limited the
DCS. Light GPU work that followed heavier work therefore ran without the
floor for up to that second: on Neo, compute.light d1 took 148-151 us
instead of 102.7 us for the first 0.5-0.75 s of a run, before the floor
came back.

Under sustained heavy rendering the PMP limits the DCS again within much
less than that, so a 100 ms hold keeps the floor released there while the
light work that follows gets it back almost at once.

(cherry picked from commit 51350a0d50224a07399bc69fc85660f781538814)
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/soc/apple/clpc.c | 9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/clpc.c b/drivers/soc/apple/clpc.c
index 97a7e88875..915dea8095 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/clpc.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/clpc.c
@@ -55,8 +55,13 @@ enum apple_clpc_rail {
 #define APPLE_CLPC_BUSY_PCT	50
 /* Time a rail keeps its last request after demand stops */
 #define APPLE_CLPC_HOLD_MS	32
-/* Time the GPU floor stays released after the PMP last limited the DCS */
-#define APPLE_CLPC_GPU_BACKOFF_MS	1000
+/*
+ * Time the GPU floor stays released after the PMP last limited the DCS. Under
+ * sustained heavy rendering the PMP limits the DCS again well within this, so
+ * the floor stays released; light submissions that follow get the floor back
+ * this long after the PMP stops limiting.
+ */
+#define APPLE_CLPC_GPU_BACKOFF_MS	100
 
 /**
  * struct apple_clpc_hw - per-SoC CLPC parameters
